  "query_preview": "..\n\nSeasonably strong ridging continues today and will bring\nabove average temperatures and dry conditions into the region. \nThrough this afternoon and evening, the ridge will begin to \nflatten out from a developing low over Alberta, Canada. The \npressure gradient in response will increase and winds aloft \nstrengthen. Assuming efficient mixing from warm temperatures, \nbreezy and gusty winds are likely through tonight. \n\nThe presence of a chinook arch may help to further increase \ntemperatures and drying conditions today as downsloping winds \ncompressionally warm and increase ground temperatures. Fire \nweather concerns slightly increase this afternoon, but current \nNDVI imagery indicates mostly green vegetation which should \ninhibit any fire growth.\n\nTemperatures on Sunday will cool off behind a passing cold front,\nthen weak ridging returns early next week ahead of a digging \nupper low off of the west coast. As the system continues to \nadvance southward, upper- level flow into Northeast Montana \ngradually shifts towards the southeast. By midweek, the low is cut\noff by a northern stream trough and begins moving inland towards \nthe Four Corners. Pacific moisture will be pulled into the \nnorthern trough and transport moisture into the CWA, increasing \nchances for precipitation late next week into the weekend. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n\n- Temperatures trending higher next week in latest NBM runs. \n\n- Given the quiet weather through the week, confidence is generally\n  high through forecast period. Uncertainty remains with any \n  precipitation at the end of next week.\n\n- Confidence of storm track late next week is low. Beyond Thursday,\n  model agreement decreases on the disturbance's path over the \n  Four Corners region. Currently, the GFS20 favors a northerly \n  track into Montana, while the ECMWF- HiRes keeps it well to the \n  south. \n\nKENZ",
